/encyclopedia/pierre-de-castelnau"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pierre-de-castelnau.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre de Castelnau</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre de Castelnau was a Cistercian monk and papal legate sent by Pope Innocent III to combat Catharism in Languedoc.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-de-la-baume"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pierre-de-la-baume.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre de La Baume</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre de La Baume was bishop of Geneva from 1522 and the last bishop to reside in the city.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-de-rigaud-marquis-de-vaudreuil-cavagnial"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pierre-de-rigaud-marquis-de-vaudreuil-cavagnial.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre de Rigaud, Marquis de Vaudreuil-Cavagnial</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre de Rigaud, Marquis de Vaudreuil-Cavagnial, was the last governor general of New France, from 1755 to 1760, and the first born in Canada.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-deval"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Deval</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Deval was the French consul in Algiers in the 1820s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-dugua-de-mons"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pierre-dugua-de-mons.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Dugua de Mons</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Dugua de Mons was a Huguenot merchant and nobleman from Saintonge who held the French royal monopoly of the fur trade in North America.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-gaultier-de-varennes-sieur-de-la-verendrye"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pierre-gaultier-de-varennes-sieur-de-la-verendrye.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Gaultier de Varennes, sieur de La Vérendrye</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Gaultier de Varennes, sieur de La Vérendrye, was a French-Canadian officer, fur trader and explorer.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-laval"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pierre-laval.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Laval</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Laval was a French politician who served as prime minister in the 1930s and became the leading figure of collaboration in Vichy France.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-le-baud"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pierre-le-baud.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Le Baud</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Le Baud was a Breton priest and historian in the service of the dukes of Brittany and of Anne of Brittany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-le-moyne-diberville"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pierre-le-moyne-diberville.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Le Moyne d'Iberville</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Le Moyne d'Iberville was a soldier and explorer born in New France.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-mendes-france"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pierre-mendes-france.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Mendès France</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Mendès France was a French Radical politician and prime minister from June 1954 to February 1955.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-montet"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pierre-montet.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Montet</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Montet was a French Egyptologist.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-nkurunziza"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pierre-nkurunziza.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Nkurunziza</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Nkurunziza was a Hutu former rebel commander of the CNDD-FDD who became president of Burundi in 2005, after the civil war.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-pigneau-de-behaine"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pierre-pigneau-de-behaine.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Pigneau de Behaine</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Pigneau de Behaine was a French Catholic missionary and titular bishop of Adran who supported Nguyen Anh in his war against the Tay Son.</span></a></li></ul>
